  "justification": "Chosen to make central differences affordable while exercising every Phase B code path. MEASURED: the forward costs 1.93 s, so the 8-epsilon x 4-probe central-difference sweep is ~2 minutes per layer. It performs 4 EQS solves (1 pre-loop + 3 in-march re-solves at [25.0, 50.0, 75.0] s), so the sigma(T) coupling VJP and the adjoint restart at re-solve boundaries are both exercised more than once. The part-mean melt fraction is 0.6695 with 437 of 3091 nodes strictly inside the melt window, so the melt front sits INSIDE the part: the clip subgradients are live (which is the whole reason the 1e-5 subgradient standard exists) rather than the objective being saturated at 0 or 1 everywhere. Deviations from the Phase A anchor are deliberate and named: a coarse mesh, dt_s = 0.5 s (still CFL-stable, dt_stable = 2.177 s, n_substeps = 1), and a 2x power density so melt onset arrives in 100 s. The gate validates the DISCRETE gradient of the DISCRETE forward, so a coarser discretization is a valid gate case; it is NOT a physics claim and no Phase A number is restated from it.",
  "representativeness_limits": "single shape (circle), single mesh, coupling ON only. The coupling-OFF path is covered by the same code with the schedule inert, and that inertness is a flag-off bit-identity check, not an FD gate."
